WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.380
Consider a segment 'AB'.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:09.200
How would the perpendicular bisector of this segment look?

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:13.040
As the name says, it has to be perpendicular.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.040
And it has to bisect this segment.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.240
Let line 'L' be the perpendicular bisector of this segment

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:24.220
and say it intersects 'AB' at point 'C'.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:26.660
This will be a right angle.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:31.180
And length of 'AC' will equal length of 'BC'.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.720
The perpendicular bisector of a segment

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.860
divides that segment into two equal parts

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:39.740
and is also perpendicular to it.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:45.100
Let's erase this and look at perpendicular bisectors in a triangle.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:48.320
Consider triangle 'PQR'.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:52.600
If we draw a perpendicular bisector of each side of a triangle,

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.960
in all we will have three of them.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:00.000
The angle bisectors of all the angles of a triangle

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.320
always meet at a point.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.280
But do the perpendicular bisectors of the sides of a triangle

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.400
also meet at a single point?

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:13.620
Let line 'L' be the perpendicular bisector of segment 'PQ'.

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:18.720
These two sides will be equal and this will be a right angle.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:23.440
Let line 'M' be the perpendicular bisector of side 'QR'.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.160
That will make these two sides equal

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:28.620
and this angle a right angle.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:33.260
Now we have to see if the perpendicular bisector of segment 'PR'

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.600
passes through this point.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.280
Let line 'N' be the perpendicular bisector of segment 'PR'

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:43.800
and yes, it passes through the same point.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.240
This side will equal this side

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:50.060
and line 'N' will be perpendicular to 'PR'.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:53.320
So even the perpendicular bisectors of the triangle

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:56.220
meet at point 'C', one single point.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.280
What is this point called?

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.820
It's called the circumcenter.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.000
We have seen that the incenter of the triangle

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.860
will always lie inside the triangle.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:10.880
But does the circumcentre always lie inside the triangle?

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.860
We see that in the next part of the video.

